From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Grische <github@grische.xyz>
Date: Mon, 10 Aug 2026 09:08:49 +0000
Subject: MIPS: dts: intel-mips: describe the switch macros as DSA switches

Describe the hardware. Each macro is a DSA switch: a port per
front-panel socket carrying its label, a CPU port pointing at the conduit
the die wires it to, the MDIO bus and the firmware loader as children with
no window of their own, and one child per integrated PHY holding the
offset of its firmware address register pair and the reset line the loader
holds it on. The two macros are two trees, because a conduit carries
exactly one CPU port and nothing forwards between the macros in hardware,
so a bridge spanning both would blackhole. The manager names its eleven
windows, its interrupts and its pools, and the two conduits are children
of it: a conduit owns no register of its own, and there is one per macro.
The DMA controllers keep a window and an interrupt, and nothing else,
because nothing else about them is board data.

The window the vendor device tree calls sbim is not carried over. Nothing
maps it, nothing reads it, and no register description for its address
exists in the vendor sources, so it cannot be named; the binding says the
same.

Front-panel LAN-N reaches switch port 6-N. The board wires the sockets in
the opposite order to the port numbering and these labels are the only
record of it. The fifth PHY, on the die variant that has one, carries the
socket on the second macro, whose port is numbered 15 while its PHY
answers at address 1.

Every PHY node spells out its identifier, 0xd565a409 on every core, so
the PHY device is created from the device tree alone. These cores answer
their identification registers before the firmware the SoC loads into
them has finished booting, and a bus scan could sample a core that is
only half awake.

The manager's standard and jumbo segment pools are no-map reservations
at 0x23800000 and 0x24a00000, ending at 0x25200000, and the manager
names them std-pool and jumbo-pool. Both have to lie inside the
manager's bus-master window, so every board that includes seale_avm.dtsi
has to declare memory up to 0x25200000, and each of the three does.

Each PHY child names two reset cells, the request bit and the status bit,
and on this SoC those are different numbers. RCU_RST_STAT is a remap of
RCU_RST_REQ rather than a mirror of it, so a second cell that repeats the
first watches an unrelated bit and can only ever time out. The per-domain
map is spelled out at the reset controller node, and the five integrated
PHY lines take their status bits from it: request 29 reports in 28, 28 in
23, 26 in 22, 25 in 25, and the fifth core's request bit 31 reports in 30
- bit 31 of the status word being the watchdog reset cause, which has no
request counterpart at all.

The reset map at the reset controller gains a row for each of the five
integrated PHY lines, named by the gphy@ child that holds it. Its note
on the two reset causes now says that neither is the status of the line
whose request bit sits at the same index.

